      <article class="prose">
        <h1>Simple Ways to Prevent Clogged Drains at Home</h1>
        <p style="color:var(--color-text-muted);">
          <time datetime="2025-09-12">September 12, 2025</time>
        </p>

        <p>
          Clogged drains are one of the most common plumbing calls. Many of them
          build up slowly from everyday habits. A few consistent practices can
          reduce how often you need professional clearing.
        </p>

        <h2>What Usually Causes Clogs</h2>
        <p>
          In kitchens, grease and food particles are the main culprits. Even with
          a disposal, oils can coat pipe walls and trap other debris. In bathrooms,
          hair, soap scum, and products that do not dissolve fully create
          restrictions over time.
        </p>
        <p>
          Main lines can also collect roots or foreign objects, but household
          prevention still helps the fixtures you use every day.
        </p>

        <h2>Habits That Help</h2>
        <h3>Kitchen</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Wipe grease into the trash instead of rinsing it down the sink.</li>
          <li>Use a simple strainer to catch food scraps.</li>
          <li>Run cold water when using the disposal, and avoid fibrous or starchy waste when possible.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Bathroom</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Use a drain hair catcher in showers and tubs.</li>
          <li>Avoid flushing wipes, even those labeled flushable—they often do not break down like toilet paper.</li>
          <li>Limit how much hair and product residue goes down the drain.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>When to Call for Help</h2>
        <p>
          If multiple fixtures drain slowly, or you hear gurgling, the issue may
          be farther down the line. Persistent problems after basic clearing are
          a good time to schedule professional service. Early attention can
          prevent a full backup.
        </p>
        <p>
          Regular care will not eliminate every clog, but it meaningfully reduces
          how often drains become a crisis.
        </p>

